<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dasht-i Qipchaq</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Desht-i Kipchak, Kipchak Steppe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dasht-i Qipchaq, &quot;the Kipchak steppe&quot;, was the Persian name for the great grassland stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and Ural rivers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. It took its name from the Kipchak nomads who dominated it from the eleventh century. After the Mongol conquest it formed the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries its eastern part was contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> and Kazakh khanates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dasht-i-qipchaq.jpg" alt="Dasht-i Qipchaq" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ca. 1550 painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Padishah_(Emperor)_of_Dast-i_Qipchaq%22._Tabriz_or_Qavin,_circa_1550._British_Museum,_1948-10-9-056.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
